abstract: >-
  Voblint is a machine-checked Isabelle/HOL framework for building, running
  and verifying interprocedural abstract interpreters, modeled on the
  Goblint static analyzer. Like Goblint, it separates the flow-sensitive state
  kept at each program point from values shared across program points. It
  chains verified compilation of a small imperative language to a control-flow
  graph that records procedure calls, a semantics that follows one procedure
  call at a time, executable equation generation, and an existing verified
  equation solver into one soundness statement about the analyzer's own
  output. The statement covers every configuration the tool offers: any
  cooperating combination of five numeric analyses and a relational order
  analysis, five rules for updating shared values, three ways of
  distinguishing calling contexts (none, entry state, call string), and
  flow-sensitive or flow-insensitive program globals. It is stated about the
  same function that is exported to OCaml and runs in the command-line tool
  and the browser playground. Solver termination is a per-program premise;
  lexing, parsing, code generation and rendering lie outside the proved
  chain.
keywords:
  - abstract interpretation
  - static analysis
  - formal verification
  - Isabelle/HOL
  - interprocedural analysis
  - context sensitivity
  - Goblint
  - code generation
